      "Most email triage tools sort messages. elpino reads them, understands intent, and drafts the next step for you.\n\nInstead of a clean inbox, you get a short brief: who needs a reply, what they want, and a suggested response ready to approve.\n\nSorting was the wrong goal all along. A filter that files a message under Updates has not helped you; it has merely moved the pile. The actual unit of work in email is not the message — it is the action hiding inside it. A customer asking whether their renewal covers onboarding is really a billing question plus a calendar task. An invoice from a contractor is really a payment to schedule plus a bookkeeping note. When you treat email as a pile of messages, triage means shuffling. When you treat it as a stream of intents, triage becomes resolution.\n\nIntent detection is the core of how elpino reads your inbox. For every incoming message, it extracts four things: who is asking, what they want, by when, and how much it matters given who they are to your business. A reply from your largest customer asking for a security questionnaire ranks differently than a newsletter about productivity, obviously — but so does a quiet request from a longtime user buried in your promotions tab, which is exactly the kind of message humans drop. The scoring is tuned to your context: your VIPs, your open deals, your active projects. It gets sharper as it watches what you reply to fast and what you leave to rot.\n\nThe output is a brief, not a folder. Here is a real example of what that looks like: Acme Corp's ops lead needs the SOC 2 summary by Thursday; draft attached, two edits suggested. Your contractor's March invoice arrived; matched to the purchase order, ready to schedule. Three candidate intros from last week's webinar; two look relevant, one is a stretch. That is thirty seconds of reading for what used to be forty minutes of inbox archaeology. Every item in the brief carries the original message one tap away, because a summary you cannot verify is just a rumor with formatting.\n\nDrafting is where the time savings get dramatic. For any message that deserves a reply, elpino writes one in your voice and holds it in review. Not a generic template — a response that answers the actual question, references the actual context, and matches the register of the thread. You approve it, tweak it, or toss it, and it goes out through your own Gmail so the thread stays intact. From the recipient's perspective nothing changed. From yours, replying became a decision instead of a writing exercise.\n\nThe system is deliberately conservative about what it touches. Newsletters, notifications, automated receipts, and marketing blasts are summarized to a single line in a digest or ignored entirely — they never become brief items. Threads you are only CC'd on get lighter treatment than threads where you are the direct recipient. And anything involving money, legal commitments, or a named VIP always escalates to the top, no matter how small the wording change that triggered it.\n\nTeams that get the most out of this set it up in minutes with three habits. First, tell elpino who your VIPs are — customers over a certain size, investors, key hires — so priority is calibrated from day one. Second, let it run for a week before judging the drafts; the writing adapts to your patterns quickly, but it needs a few days of watching how you write. Third, use the reject button with a reason attached when a draft misses — every rejection is a correction the next draft benefits from.\n\nThe inbox has been the same complaint for twenty years: too much of it, all of it demanding. What has changed is that reading and writing are no longer the bottleneck — deciding is. Hand the reading and the writing to something that never gets tired, keep the deciding for yourself, and the inbox stops being a place you live. It becomes a list of intents, most of which are already handled by the time you look.",
