    slug: "the-consent-loop",
    title: "The Consent Loop: Why Proactive AI Needs Human Approval",
    excerpt:
      "Automating background actions is high risk. We explore how we built a consensus model in Telegram that never drafts or triggers actions without your permission.",
    date: "2026-06-25",
    content:
      "Automating background actions is high risk. We explore how we built a consensus model in Telegram that never drafts or triggers actions without your permission.\n\nAutonomous agents are powerful, but when they operate directly on your email, customer accounts, or payment gateways, mistake costs are high. The Consent Loop is our answer. Rather than letting the AI execute blindly, elpino constructs drafts, proposes actions, and sends a single, interactive Telegram prompt. With a simple tap on Approve or Reject, you maintain complete control without ever having to log into a heavy administration panel.\n\nThe design problem sounds simple until you feel it in production. An agent that does nothing is a toy; an agent that does everything is a liability. Between those poles is the entire craft of making automation trustworthy, and the industry keeps getting it wrong in both directions. Fully manual review means the founder becomes a bottleneck rubber-stamping trivia until they start approving without reading — at which point the review is theater. Fully autonomous means one hallucinated decision can email your entire customer list or cancel the wrong subscription. The Consent Loop exists because both failure modes come from the same mistake: treating all actions as if they carry the same risk.\n\nThey do not, and the first thing elpino does is classify. Every proposed action is sorted into tiers by what it can affect. Read-only work — summarizing, monitoring, preparing briefs — needs no approval because it cannot damage anything. Low-risk writes — calendar buffers, internal labels, draft emails held in your own drafts folder — batch into the daily brief for one-tap review. High-risk writes — sending email to customers, moving money, modifying records in production systems — always pause for explicit consent, no matter how confident the agent is, no matter how routine the action looks. A subscription cancellation that would take a human four seconds still waits, because the cost of asking is seconds and the cost of not asking is occasionally catastrophic.\n\nThe interactive prompt itself is engineered like a product surface, because it is one. Each Telegram card shows the complete action in plain language: what will happen, to whom, through which connected account, and why the agent thinks it should happen — including the exact draft of any email verbatim, not summarized. Approve executes immediately through the sandbox. Reject discards and asks, quietly, whether the reasoning missed something. Edit opens a reply thread where you change the wording and the modified version goes out. Snooze defers to tomorrow's brief. Four buttons, no settings screen, no dashboard login, decisions made in seconds from wherever you are.\n\nConsent also has to expire, or it silently becomes its opposite. Every approval is scoped to that single action — approving one email never authorizes the next. High-risk categories can be batch-allowed for a defined window when you genuinely want to step back, but the window is explicit, visible, and revocable in one tap. And everything is logged: every proposal, every decision, every edit, with timestamps and the reasoning the agent used at the time. When you wonder a month later why a certain email went out, the full audit trail is right there.\n\nThe unglamorous lesson from months of running this in anger is that the Loop earns trust in exactly one way: by being boring and predictable, every single time. Users start by reading every card, then reading the ones that matter, then scanning, then trusting — and that progression is healthy, because at each step the option to slow back down is one tap away. Automation should never require faith. It should show its work, wait for the nod, and keep perfect minutes of every conversation it has ever had on your behalf.\n\nThat is the whole model: propose, wait, act, log. It is slower than blind autonomy and faster than doing everything yourself, and unlike both of them, it is still standing when something goes wrong — because someone human saw it first.",
