    date: "2026-06-18",
    content:
      "Learn how to configure SQL and MongoDB database monitors to feed transactional data, signups, and error rates straight to Riz.\n\nBusiness metrics shouldn't require manual querying. By linking database read-only credentials, elpino runs lightweight background queries on signups, high-value payments, or system exceptions. Instead of configuring complex reporting pipelines, Riz interprets query outputs and notes anomalies directly within your daily Telegram summary. You get the pulse of your production databases without the visual clutter.\n\nThe problem with dashboards is that they are rooms you have to walk into. Your signups, your revenue movement, your error rates — all of it sits behind a login, arranged in charts someone must remember to visit. This guide walks through connecting your databases so the reverse happens: the numbers come to you, in plain language, only when there is something worth saying.\n\nStart with credentials, and start strict. Create a dedicated read-only user for elpino in your database — on PostgreSQL, a role with CONNECT and SELECT on the schemas you want watched, and nothing else; on MongoDB, a user whose role is read on the specific database, never readWrite, never clusterAdmin. The connection wizard in the elpino dashboard walks through this, and the credentials are encrypted at rest with keys isolated from the agent runtime, the same architecture we use for every integration. Elpino never writes to your database. There is no path for it to do so, which is the only way to make this feature boring.\n\nNext, define what to watch. Rather than asking you to write SQL, elpino starts from a small set of built-in monitors that cover what most founders actually need to know. New signups per day, with a spotlight on any account that looks like an organization rather than a hobby. Payment events, filtered by value so a $200 monthly subscription failing stands out from a $9 one. Error and exception counts from your application tables, trending over the last day against the last month. Each monitor is a lightweight scheduled query — think seconds of execution, run a few times a day — and you can edit the underlying query directly if you want something custom. The only rule is read-only.\n\nThe part that makes this genuinely useful is the interpretation layer. Raw query output is how machines see data; Riz translates it into how a founder sees it. The daily brief does not say count 3, average 41.2. It says signups dipped to a third of the usual Tuesday rate, the biggest drop is from paid search, and one enterprise trial started where they have already invited four teammates. When a metric crosses the anomaly band — each monitor learns a baseline, so a quiet Saturday spiking to Wednesday levels gets flagged while ordinary noise does not — the note lands in the brief with the underlying numbers one tap away, so trust is always verifiable.\n\nA few practical tips from deployments we have watched. Point monitors at replicas or analytics databases if your production primaries are sensitive to load; the queries are light, but there is no reason to spend production headroom on them. Start with fewer monitors than you think you need — three good ones beat thirty skimmable ones. And resist monitoring vanity numbers: elpino will happily watch total registered users climbing forever, but that metric has never once required a decision. Watch the things that, when they move, mean you should do something.\n\nThe whole setup takes about ten minutes: create the read-only user, paste the connection string, pick your monitors, choose when the summary should arrive. From the next morning on, the question of how the database is doing stops being a task and becomes a line you read with your coffee.",
