    slug: "oauth-token-isolation-security",
    title: "Security and OAuth Token Isolation in LLM Agents",
    excerpt:
      "Running AI over workspace data demands absolute key isolation. Here is how we isolate user tokens from the execution runtime.",
    date: "2026-06-12",
    content:
      "Running AI over workspace data demands absolute key isolation. Here is how we isolate user tokens from the execution runtime.\n\nTraditional integrations store tokens alongside execution logs. In the elpino architecture, credentials for Gmail, Stripe, and Slack are encrypted and kept in isolated storage. The LLM agent receives only localized context and does not have access to the credentials. When a tool call is generated, it is validated in an isolated execution sandbox that merges the decrypted token, processes the API request, and immediately wipes the keys from active memory.\n\nStart with the threat model, because every design decision downstream comes from it. If an AI agent orchestrates your email, payments, and chat, then the most valuable target in the system is not the agent — it is the credentials that let anything act as you. A prompt injection that merely confuses the model is recoverable. A prompt injection that leaks a Gmail refresh token is not. So the question we asked was blunt: can the LLM, under any circumstances, no matter how it is manipulated, get its hands on a credential? The architecture answers no, structurally.\n\nThe first separation is storage. Tokens issued by OAuth providers are encrypted with per-tenant keys and held in a dedicated credential store that has exactly one job: custody. It does not run models. It does not execute tools. It speaks one protocol — a signed request asking for a short-lived grant to call a specific provider on behalf of a specific user. The LLM runtime cannot query it directly, read its contents, or even ask what providers a grant covers. From the model's perspective, credentials do not exist. It knows that tools exist, and that is all.\n\nThe second separation is execution. When the model decides an action is needed — send this email, create this event — it does not perform an API call. It emits a structured tool call: provider, operation, parameters. That structure passes through a validator that checks it against the user's connected permissions and the consent rules, and only then reaches the execution sandbox. The sandbox is a short-lived isolated process that does three things: requests the grant from the credential store, makes the provider API call, and returns only the result — never the token — to the orchestrator. The decrypted credential exists in memory for the duration of one HTTP request and is then overwritten. There is no point in the flow where a secret and the model share an address space.\n\nScope discipline carries the same weight as isolation. Elpino requests the narrowest OAuth scopes that make a feature function, and the validator enforces them at call time — a grant for reading calendar cannot send email, even if a malformed or malicious tool call asks it to. Provider-side, we recommend users connect with scoped accounts where their workspace supports it, so the blast radius of any credential is bounded by design rather than by hope.\n\nLogging closes the loop, and logging is where careless systems leak. Execution logs record what was done: operation, parameters, outcome, latency. They never record what was used to do it. Token prefixes, authorization headers, and connection strings are scrubbed before anything is written. The audit trail is rich enough to answer what elpino did with your Gmail in March, and useless to anyone hoping to find a key in it.\n\nNone of this is exotic — short-lived grants, isolated executors, scope validation are all known techniques. What is uncommon is applying them to AI agents with the same seriousness as payment infrastructure, and that is exactly the standard we hold elpino to. If a system is going to act on your behalf, the first thing it must prove is that it cannot act as you. The credentials stay locked away, the agent never sees them, and the only thing that ever leaves the sandbox is the result you asked for.",
