    date: "2026-04-29",
    content:
      "A step-by-step walkthrough on linking payment gateways to monitor failed payments, recurring subs, and revenue anomalies.\n\nRevenue events are critical signals. By hooking Stripe or Razorpay endpoints into elpino, our operator scans for billing events. If a recurring subscription fails twice, or a high-tier customer experiences checkout abandonment, Riz drafts an email in Gmail offering support and schedules it for your review. This turns checkout monitoring into automated revenue recovery.\n\nPayment webhooks are the highest-value signal most businesses ignore. The events arrive faithfully — payment failed, subscription renewed, invoice past due — and then they sit in a dashboard tab nobody has open. This walkthrough connects that stream to an operator who actually reads it, and turns the two most expensive event types into recoverable revenue.\n\nFirst, the connection. In your Stripe or Razorpay dashboard, create a webhook endpoint pointing at your elpino ingestion URL — the dashboard generates it, along with the signing secret. Paste the secret back into elpino so every event is verified on arrival; unsigned or mismatched events are dropped, which is the webhook equivalent of not opening the door for strangers. Select the event types that matter: payment failures, subscription lifecycle changes, refund events, and — for Razorpay — subscription halt and resume. Avoid subscribing to everything just in case; irrelevant events cost attention later.\n\nSecond, tell elpino which events deserve a reaction. The default policy covers the patterns that actually lose money. A payment failure is a note in the brief. Two consecutive failures on the same subscription is a flag, because that is the documented pattern of a card that will not recover on its own. A failure or abandonment on a checkout above your high-value threshold — you set the number — is immediate, even outside the brief, because a $4,000 annual plan that bounced at the payment step is a fire, not a metric.\n\nThird, the recovery flow, which is where the operator earns its keep. When a subscription fails twice, elpino assembles context before drafting anything: who the customer is, how long they have been paying, what tier, whether support has an open thread with them. It drafts the dunning email in your voice — short, human, specific to their situation, with the exact reason the charge failed and a working update-payment link — and queues it for your approval. You approve, edit, or reject in one tap; the send goes out through your own Gmail so the thread lives in your actual relationship with the customer, not in a dunning service's black box.\n\nCheckout abandonment gets the gentler treatment. A high-value prospect who stopped at the payment step gets a drafted check-in that offers help — is something not going through, can we get a different payment method on file — sent once, reviewed by you first. Persistence beyond one follow-up is exactly the kind of thing that turns a recovery email into a spam complaint, so the operator will not do it.\n\nReliability notes for the operators among you. Webhooks retry automatically on your provider's schedule when they fail to deliver, and elpino processes every event exactly once — idempotency keys are recorded on arrival, so a replayed webhook updates rather than duplicates. Events are also reconciled daily against the gateway's own records, so if a webhook was silently dropped days ago, the evening brief mentions the gap instead of letting it rot. Billing state should never depend on remembering whether the pipe held.\n\nThe result of this setup is a change in kind, not degree. Failed payments stop being a statistic you check after the month closes and become work items that arrive pre-researched with a draft answer attached. The gateway watches the money; the operator watches the relationship around the money; you make the small number of calls that need judgment.",
